Z||000000||87FieroGTx||09-02-2001||09:28 PM|||| I have a 85GT Auto....<BR> <BR> OK.. So I tried to remove the alternator from the bottom but there's NO WAY IN HELL its coming out that way without major sergery!<P> So how hard is it from the top? From what I hear all you do is Unbolt the DogBone tilt the engine back and remove the coolent tube and it slips out? What about the bracket that holds the Alternator? Won't that get in the way?||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||09-02-2001||09:47 PM|||| <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013234.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/013234.html</A> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||87FieroGTx||09-03-2001||03:22 PM|||| Hmm.. Now it seems that the car drains the battery DEAD even when its not running?<P> I hooked up a brand new battery and over night it drained it dead. Anyone have any Idea what's up?<P><BR> EDIT: The "BAD?" Alternator is still in.<p>[This message has been edited by 87FieroGTx (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000003||cscougar||09-03-2001||03:39 PM||||I blocked up my 85 GT and then put the floor jack under the cradle crossmember in the rear and loosened and removed the two rear cradle bolts.  Then I lowered the cradle as far as the dog bone would allow.  Left plenty of room to remove and install the alternator.  After finishing the jack up the cradle and install the two cradle bolts.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||09-03-2001||06:37 PM||||yep, it was the alternator. Mine went bad, internal short and drained my battery too. I put in new alternator with same battery 2 years ago now.<P>I really never paid attention to stock exhaust, mine is turbo so exhaust is different I guess, cause i take mine right out the bottom. takes less than 20 minutes start to finish. I just take out top pivot bolt, belt adjustment bolt, wires and cooling tube and it falls on the floor. V6, 4 speed.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 09-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||voyagerspe||09-03-2001||11:54 PM||||couger has the right idea.  This is the best way that I have found.  It helps if you use an impact wrench on the cradle bolts though.  <BR>goodluck<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000006||87FieroGTx||09-04-2001||12:08 AM|||| Ok.. I'm almost done!!!!<P> I removed the Dogbone and bracket, Cooling tube and pulled it out while pushing on the engine VERY EASY!! The only prob I had was  the bolts where the cooling tube mounts got in the way so I had to push them in.<P> I just Droped in the new Alternator hooked it to the tension mount thingy <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but it doesnt want to lign up? What I think I need to do is Hook up the Dogbone so its all in the right place. We will see tomarrow!<P> Thx for all the help I'll prob need more <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000007||87FieroGTx||09-04-2001||12:39 AM||||!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!200POSTS!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||4
Z||000008||AL||09-04-2001||12:40 AM||||the only thing i did was to take the tire off, remove the pulley, then i took it out of the bottom,then i did the reverse and presto,nothing else to remove at all.<BR><B>AL</B> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/AL_sig.gif"> <BR>84 Indy 4 speed(stole it)250$<BR>86 Gt rebuilt it<BR>89 WS6 GTA<P>------------------<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||87FieroGTx||09-04-2001||12:47 AM|||| It seems like alternator removal in Manuals are a LOT EASIER than in Auto's.<BR> <BR> Love the car AL!||||reg||2
